															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/hewhowalksbefore" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5227776); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5227776&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200816" alt="HeWhoWalksBefore" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5227776)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/hewhowalksbefore" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5227776); return false;" >HeWhoWalksBefore</a></span> <span class="odate time_1575844852 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">08 Dec 2019 22:40</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4441432">
									

<p>Alright, so I'm writing this as I read to keep track of thoughts.</p>
<ul>
<li>First off, why is the fact that the light is the byproduct of something redacted? Right now without more info it just seems like you couldn't think of something to put there.</li>
<li>While I like the fact that the d class and the staff are talking like normal people, one gripe is that they record the fact that Mai stifled a laugh. Doesn't seem like something relevant, especially with the note right above it about removing irrelevant communication. It does however change the attitude for the next line where Mai scolds the D-Class into something less harsh, so I can understand the inclusion.</li>
</ul>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>I like the fact that we can't rely on the transcripts, while not a new idea it typically interests me. Especially when it makes me question which D-26 or D-99 is the real one, did he become effected by the light and is the one muttering strange things or is he the one begging to be returned? Are either of them original?</li>
<li>Alright, at the end. I really dislike the movement of Dr. Mai to the new SCP. While I completely expected her to be disciplined for her behavior, I can't believe that after showing that she couldn't handle this job where she lost 2 D-class, she would be put into an apparently dangerous position in charge of MORE D-Class. It's like immersion therapy but it could actually harm the patient AND other people. Not to mention is seems cruel rather than cold. The foundation has tons of positions, and typically when I read someone being moved because of a similar situation they are moved to a place where they don't have as much chance of fucking things up. Pushing this woman like they seem to do will likely not make her &quot;gain a deeper understanding of how the Foundation utilizes our D-Class personnel&quot; (What?!), but make her less stable, possibly turning her into a security risk. Give her a damn desk job if you have to, move her to a quiet scp, hell stick her with 999 for a week and some therapy for awhile and reevaluate her personality.</li>
</ul>
<p>Anyways, while there isn't anything particularly special about this scp, there doesn't need to be. I like the premise, I like the way you go about things sometimes, but overall I end up a bit on the negative side. I think you can make this into an article that I would upvote however, and I don't want you to think I hated it. I actually enjoyed reading it except for the last part.</p>
<p>The twist of &quot;Hiding from the first, the biggest&quot; would also probably be investigated a bit more by the Foundation. Even just a note saying something along the lines of &quot;No information found to verify claims&quot; or &quot;subsequent communication not found to be coherent or reliable&quot; should probably be added. I doubt that the Foundation would just ignore the ability to speak to the things in this anomaly even if the words can't really be trusted. More information is always a good thing.</p>
<p>Also, unless those things would come out of the anomaly, I would probably designate it as safe. Though there are sapient (maybe?) beings in it, it doesn't appear they will ever leave, but I can understand Euclid.</p>
<p>Lastly, I don't recognize Ashby Protocol, and looking it up through google got me nothing. Did they terminate all communication with the anomaly? Why? It appeared that nobody outside the anomaly was in danger. Unless Mai was under the affect of a cognitohazard, in which case it should be stated.</p>
<p>I know this was a wall of mostly negative text, sorry about that. But take it as you grabbing my interest enough for me to care this much. Also, feel free to point out any errors in my understanding.</p>
<p>Edit: Perhaps a note that the threats heard about the *Biggest one* are thought to be simply another form of lure to make people enter the light? I also read the greenlight thread, and understand a bit more now. However I would double down on the fact that the cause of the light in the center should not be redacted. While it does kind of give away what will happen to the D-Class, if this were an actual report you would not redact the cause of the light to build tension. Perhaps make it vague, such as the light is a byproduct of the multitude of compressed objects approaching the center.</p>
<p>Edit Edit: I noticed in the page at the bottom you addressed the message to &quot;To: Dr. Dr. Mai Giang Lê&quot;. I removed the second Dr. for you.</p>
</div>
