<p>I very much appreciate the feedback. You had good points, allow me to make a few brief responses.</p>
<p><strong>1)</strong> I do know what's behind the black boxes, and the logs hint at what it is as well. The &quot;big secret&quot; here is that as individuals (and their instances) become smaller and more compact (so very small, so very close) as they approach the center, they would appear from the outside to be a light. Just as well, those tiny, tiny, <em>tiny</em> instances would then become the dust itself, meaning they were wiping <em>people</em> from their camera lenses. I was inspired by 2719 to write a &quot;mystery SCP&quot; that had a definite answer, but required a bit of work to figure it out. A couple of reviewers were able to do so, which is why I felt confident enough to post it to the site. It would be redacted by the Foundation because the ethics of that whole situation would likely complicate the relatively easy containment they're managing already.</p>
<p><strong>2)</strong> That letter has been more divisive than anything. The original version had an extra paragraph that spelled this out a bit, but the Foundation moving Mai to an SCP that &quot;eats&quot; D-class seems to be a very Foundationy move to me. They don't want to kill Mai, or inhibit her ability to work, so instead, they stick her with an SCP that would force her to treat D-class expendably. Would she put up a fight? Maybe, but on the other hand, this would be pretty classic conditioning via immersion therapy, which is right up the Foundation's alley. It would be a nightmare for Mai.</p>
<p><strong>3)</strong> &quot;The first&quot; is my coy reference that this SCP is a safe room from 001, whatever that might be. I didn't want to directly cross-link to 001 on my first article, as I didn't want to piggyback success, but that's the idea behind it. It's also why the Foundation would not be crazy about publicly investigating this much further, since they likely have a good idea of what that danger is already.</p>
<p><strong>4)</strong> The Ashby Protocol is something I made up after being offered no existing solutions. Basically, it's the idea that if you no longer can be certain you're talking to Foundation personnel, you cut bait. That seems to be in line with what a paranoid, secret organization would do. I'm open to suggestions, but I tried to imply the definition without being lame and outright stating it.</p>
<p>I very much appreciate your feedback. I'm thinking I might just need to give away what the &quot;big secret&quot; of this thing is so people understand that yes, there is an answer to the questions I've asked here.</p>
<p>Also, I appreciate the edit.</p>
<p>Thank you much.</p>

							</div>

								
