			<div class="content" id="post-content-4441464">
									

<p><strong>1)</strong> While I'm still not sold on the redaction, I understand it a bit more. The idea that the people are the dust makes this darker and more interesting too. I don't see how people knowing about what the scp does would complicate the containment, they are not doing this the anomaly is, and ethically I don't see any reason why they should risk people trying to fix the anomaly unless they plan to use it. I don't think it would compromise the containment at the very least.</p>
<p>I imagine there to be a way to reverse the dust back to people then if this is supposed to be a safe room? Or maybe secret research into turning it into a safe room?</p>
<p><strong>2)</strong> I can agree with the fact that the Foundation would not want to inhibit Mai's ability to work, but by sticking her in a MORE traumatic place I would think that it would increase the likelihood of her being unable to perform. Rather than taking that risk I think they would move her to a quieter place, perhaps try again later. And the fact that you state that &quot;It would be a nightmare for Mai&quot; does not fit with my idea of the Foundation. I like that tagline, &quot;Cold but not Cruel&quot;. There is no purpose in needless suffering, and the Foundation is supposed to prevent needless suffering. That whole, &quot;we die in the dark so you can live in the light&quot; thing. Ultimately I don't think they would risk wasting a promising researcher.</p>
<p><strong>3)</strong> It doesn't really seem like a &quot;Safe place&quot; to hide from whatever 001 proposal you are referring to, but it is an anomaly and so it's weird. While I know I don't connect dots as well as other people, I don't think there is really enough here to indicate the connection to 001. My first thought was that it was an indication of some out-of-reality being, perhaps a malevolent god or something like scp 3125. Which I guess is actually along the lines of what you were hoping for, so maybe I just didn't connect the last step.</p>
<p><strong>4)</strong> Alright, that's reasonable. Personally, I could see the Foundation doing a little bit more testing through &quot;Interviews&quot; to extract more data, but given the idea behind it I guess it would go against the purpose.</p>
<p>I don't think I'm going to upvote this right now, honestly I'm not really a fan of the Foundation stopping testing like they did because they want to use this as a secret safe room. But I will remove my downvote, I can see you put a lot of effort into this and it does come together a bit more now.</p>

