			<div class="content" id="post-content-4441467">
									

<p>1) I think that they’re happier saying, “Those people are gone, there are no Foundation personnel inside the SCP” than they are admitting that there might be living people who could conceivably be rescued. They don’t exactly know what happens in there per se, but they aren’t interested in debating whether they should attempt to extract them, which is exactly what Mai tried to do.</p>
<p>2) From the Foundation’s view, this wouldn’t be cruel. She has to learn to deal with this to succeed in the Foundation; they can’t simply have her as an ethical live wire. They have justified reasons for using expendable people in tests and maintenance. Mai comes from “the real world” where this would be absurd, but the Foundation doesn’t work by regular ethics. They need her to be comfortable sending people to their deaths, and perhaps forcing her to do it over and over would sharpen her into the researcher they want. It’s absurd to us, but it would be fairly logical for them, even if we might disagree whether it’s effective or not.</p>
<p>3) This is largely the reason why I didn’t make a direct 001 connection. It’s simply implied that it’s something big and scary, or at least 99 believes so. Exactly what that is might be the subject of an established or new SCP, but this article focuses on the “safe room” that seems to exist to counter it.</p>
<p>4) I didn’t mean to imply that the Foundation has any desire to use this SCP. They don’t know exactly what it is. They know that 99, or something claiming to be 99, thinks it’s a safe place, but they’re not going to march anyone else in there.</p>
<p>Hope this clears a few things up. I appreciate you working through your doubts with me. I’m getting the impression that others might be struggling to piece the puzzle together as well. I wrote this for people like me who love these SCPs that make you work a bit for the answer.</p>
<p>Edit: I removed the redacted sentence about the byproduct. It’s causing more problems than it’s worth.</p>

