			<div class="content" id="post-content-4441474">
									

<p>1) I imagine the D-class are the same for me as anyone else; they’re expendable test subjects. But they’re still people. I didn’t want to use an MTF or agent to explore an unknown SCP with no clear threat, but I did want someone who could operate a bit more competently. Hence, the PET: a team of two D-class who have survived more than a couple of other SCPs and seem to have a rapport with their researcher. I wanted to show what happens when the Foundation fails to properly indoctrinate their doctors into treating humans inhumanely. It’s not so much a war as it is simply a doctor who ended up caring for a couple of her test subjects.</p>
<p>2) The redactions seem to be contentious. The reason is that if they acknowledge the possibility that there are living people inside, that might obligate them in some way. As it stands, they can’t confirm that anyone is actually alive in there, or that there aren’t hostiles. A rescue mission would be ill-advised. But they don’t even have to deal with the controversy; they can simply claim that if they don’t know for <em>sure</em>, then they might as well keep the theory away from the rank and file to prevent any complaints about just leaving it alone.</p>
<p>Hope this clarifies. I’m really wrestling with the redactions if that’s what’s turning people off to this, but after I had a couple of people in chat guess the right answer for what was happening, I really wanted to keep this a little bit of a justified puzzle.</p>
<p>Thanks for asking, I appreciate it.</p>

