<blockquote>
<p>Hence, the PET: a team of two D-class who have survived more than a couple of other SCPs and seem to have a rapport with their researcher.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I particularly liked that bit of worldbuilding there. Seems like a fun idea, and I'd love to see it in more articles.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>But they don’t even have to deal with the controversy; they can simply claim that if they don’t know for sure, then they might as well keep the theory away from the rank and file to prevent any complaints about just leaving it alone.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I totally understand that, but I think the article would be aided by literal expungement; as in, you only allude to the truth but never explicit mark anything out. If the Ashby Protocol is simple an order of no-contact, then maybe the article could end with a final addendum, reiterating that the Protocol precludes investigation of the anomaly. That'd be rather chilling, I suppose.</p>
<p>I don't want to tell you how to write your article, since you seem like a very competent writer on your own, but it's mostly just an aggregate of small issues here that are leading me to novote. The dialogue, formatting, and clinical tone are all on-point, but I feel like there's a potentiality of this article that's being withheld. The Foundation enforcing a doctrine of ignorance to avoid complications to containment is a chilling violation of ethics and I love every bit of it; I just wish it were examined more closely by the article, I suppose.</p>
<p>Well, anyways, best of luck! I can tell you've got a pretty bright future on the site, if you can craft this on your first attempt.</p>

